# 0.4 (backlog 35, ruled 2026-08-17): the no-restatement check is
# OPERAND-SCOPED. `c` ends the `granted` branch in BOUND and the `refused`
# branch in INIT, and it entered the fragment in REQUESTING. At most one
# operand of an `alt` occurs, so the branch INIT does not restate anything —
# it is compared with REQUESTING, the most recent occurrence in a scope that
# encloses its own, and not with BOUND, which is on the path it excludes.
#
# This is the case the ruling was made for: `examples/sequence/dhcp-lease.fd`
# had to carry the DHCPNAK/DHCPDECLINE restart in a `description=` because the
# check read a lifeline's occurrences as one linear sequence.
figdown 0.4 sequence
title "Exclusive branches may end in the same state"
lifeline c "Client"
lifeline s "Server"
state c "INIT"
message c -> s "REQUEST"
state c "REQUESTING"
fragment commit "can the server satisfy it?" type=alt
operand granted "yes" in=commit
message c <- s "ACK" in=granted
state c "BOUND" in=granted
operand refused "no" in=commit
message c <- s "NAK" in=refused
state c "INIT" in=refused
